    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_002a8532648d85cc60eedfcb09c53f391cfa10a042c752425c3cc3f3050f4341",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Fitting the same finite zero spectrum by variational, dressing, and inverse-scattering procedures yields the same one-dimensional potential, and the review invokes inverse-scattering uniqueness to argue that the recovered roughness is not merely an algorithmic artifact. Disagreement in reported fractal dimension instead tracks changes in the standardized input spectrum and its long-range correlations. A single fitted dimension and a later multifractal description are different claims; neither a finite fit nor cross-method agreement establishes an asymptotic spectral explanation.",
