    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_fcbe89bf01b0007e42e6d58e7198f148b7dd2fafed9d5674014e9791471e2505",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Enough independent conserved actions freeze the action coordinates and make the angle coordinates evolve linearly, confining an integrable trajectory to an invariant torus. For the circular billiard, passing from the continuous flight to the boundary bounce map exposes the conserved incidence angle: rational rotation produces a periodic orbit, whereas irrational rotation gives dense or uniformly distributed impacts without chaotic sensitivity. Cutting a hole changes the question from closed-orbit geometry to an escape-time observable while retaining the same bounce representation.",
