    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_843c6e80b84f9ac50dd99e5420bef1ea8b45c375b8006f65af995eaac1fde0bd",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Across the surveyed constructions, reproducing coarse or preinserted zeta information is systematically easier than explaining its arithmetic fluctuations. Exact negative-curvature geometry derives an orbit-spectrum relation but its noncompact zeta scattering factor yields no control of zeros; artificial scattering maps can place zero data in several spectral roles; the xp bulk flow recovers the smooth count but moves fluctuations into unresolved boundary conditions; and fitting exact zeros transfers their long-range correlations into a rough potential. The overall trace-formula sign can even force a change from added levels to missing spectral lines, so the unresolved content may reside in boundary data, correlations, or the spectral interpretation itself.",
